Compares the distribution of factors known to predict blood lead levels (BLLs) in children for children living in neighborhoods with ULAB processing sites (Proximal
n = 67) vs. children living in neighborhoods without these sited (Distal n = 68) and Overall (n = 135).
Proximal children were more likely to live in apartments, compared to Distal children. Distal children were more likely to use cumin and locally sourced loose spices,
drink water from bore hole wells (deep artisanal wells) and be 2–4 years old. The geometric mean BLL was higher in Distal children, but the difference did not reach
statistical significance (p> 0.05).

